 "note": {
  "status": "draft",
  "text": "Data are the facts and measurements we collect in an experiment. Scientists need data as evidence: a conclusion must be based on what was observed.\n• Qualitative data describe with words: colour, smell, “the water became cloudy”.\n• Quantitative data are numbers with units: 25 °C, 3.2 cm, 45 s.\nRecord data in a table. Each column heading gives the quantity and its unit, for example “Time (s)”, so the unit is not written in every box.\nRepeat each measurement (for example three times) and find the mean: add the readings and divide by how many there are. A reading that is very different from the others is anomalous: check it, repeat it, and leave it out of the mean.",
  "checks": []
 },
 "worked_example": {
  "status": "draft",
  "vars": {
   "a": 12.4,
   "b": 12.6,
   "c": 12.5,
   "n": 3
  },
  "problem": "Bih times a ball rolling down a ramp three times: {a} s, {b} s and {c} s. Find the mean time.",
  "steps": [
   "Add the readings: {a} + {b} + {c} = {a + b + c} s.",
   "Divide by the number of readings, {n}: {a + b + c} ÷ {n} = {mean([a, b, c])} s."
  ],
  "answer": "The mean time is {mean([a, b, c])} s.",
  "checks": [
   "mean([a, b, c]) == 12.5"
  ]
 },
